  "answer_text": "Here's a breakdown of the typical jersey numbers for **linebackers** in the NFL:\n\n### Current NFL Rules (as of 2021 expansion)\nLinebackers are currently allowed to wear numbers **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**.\n\n### Most Traditional/Common Ranges\n- **50\u201359** have historically been the most classic linebacker numbers.\n- **90\u201399** are also commonly worn by linebackers (shared with defensive linemen).\n\n### How the Rules Evolved Over Time\n1. In **1984**, the NFL allowed linebackers to wear jersey numbers in the **90\u201399** range, since more teams were making use of the 3\u20134 defense and were quickly exhausting numbers for linebackers, who previously were only allowed to wear numbers in the **50\u201359** range.\n2. The NFL Competition Committee made another change in **2015**, allowing linebackers to use numbers **40\u201349** for the first time.\n3. In **2021**, NFL owners passed a rule further expanding available numbers, allowing linebackers to wear **1\u201379** and **90\u201399**. (The current official rule settles at **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**.)\n\n### Quick Reference Summary\n\n| Number Range | Linebacker Eligibility |\n|---|---|\n| 1\u201359 | \u2705 Allowed |\n| 60\u201389 | \u274c Not allowed |\n| 90\u201399 | \u2705 Allowed |\n\nSo while you'll most traditionally see linebackers in the **50s** (e.g., 51, 52, 55, 58) or **90s** (e.g., 90, 94, 98), the modern rules give them much more flexibility across a wide range of numbers.",
